class.description:
The search runs [i]backwards[/i]: it starts from the goal's desired state and works towards the current world state, considering only actions that close a condition which is still open. That keeps the branching factor tied to the size of the goal rather than to the size of the action set, so adding actions an agent rarely needs costs little.
At each step an action is kept when it satisfies at least one open condition and undoes none of the others; what it provides is then struck from the open set and what it requires is added in its place. A plan is complete once every remaining condition already holds in the world state.
Each [GoapAgent] owns a planner, so there is usually no need to create one. Doing so is still useful to inspect what an agent would decide:
[codeblock]
var planner = GoapPlanner.new()
var plan = planner.plan(agent.context, agent.actions, agent.goals)
if plan == null:
    print("No reachable goal (%d/%d nodes explored)" % [planner.get_last_iterations(), planner.max_iterations])
[/codeblock]
[b]Note:[/b] Planning is synchronous. With the default limits it is cheap enough to run from [method Node._process], but a large action set combined with a distant goal can take long enough to matter; use [member GoapAgent.replan_delay] to control how often it happens.
